#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119603 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119792 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9279d979-74ba-5504-b98a-63692db3af72" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "0e45ac96-0eee-51a3-83e0-192582dcf57d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4765" :question/problem "Given ![](images/98/98496fe489edc905b587f42825bd0ef76dffa68bece7e204abae6de259501835.png) find its LU decomposition." :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 9 \\\\ -7 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+1\\cdot {R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}7 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}7 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119604 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119793 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6093e24d-14bb-5e3b-a0b4-7a562a12f6a2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization of a Singular 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "e1d1f80c-70d1-566d-9cc0-94899b0fa05d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4767" :question/problem "Given \n![](images/4a/4a8b4b988e2f4c93881f577ce12f2aa59a4dbcc00bf4122e23873255c27c2d72.png), find its LU decomposition." :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 12 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119668 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119853 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c1a562-1b3d-5180-9fef-e20ea916b17a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying LU Factorizations"}] :question/id #uuid "ea39d5ec-9c8f-572d-8708-fdd97d0c04bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-6184" :question/problem "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $A?$\n\n- ![](images/f0/f07498a45503a02fa9fb4d3ecfd9bdae54a363b632152016f2f4e5c3f6170656.png)\n\n- ![](images/30/300278e3edb64ab9dcb945668b923f385b9873be5bd33264a7ed10f6072262b7.png)\n\n- ![](images/e2/e26513d3753cd1437a42a9f57f160fb25e2fb9b84e1df1db07579429749eea2c.png)" :question/worked-solution "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $A,$ we write $A=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n![](images/98/98d64291b8e0d4effe6bb1f3006c3d88d2687697355a83c1b092cdfb54ba4188.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n![](images/10/10a251d4445a3b190e92fa179badfb5e989b577f3c88812334e921522e7a20e5.png)\n\n- The second product is *not* in $LU$ form. Although the left matrix is a lower triangular matrix, it is not a *unit* lower triangular matrix because it does not have $1$'s on the diagonal.\n![](images/51/515f139b62a15ee4b38066e6225066b527e277e904ea97473d2a9f50f0f595cb.png)\n\n- The third product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the right is not an upper triangular matrix, as it has a non-zero entry below the diagonal.\n![](images/e3/e377b0ff63ac6d7b3938187e47a3b5bca0087ddb6eee1d2c086835f394cdf6b9.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243950 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119792 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9279d979-74ba-5504-b98a-63692db3af72" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00fcd0e3-1b06-5b0d-8ec5-bdcb973e0121" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241193} {:answer/id #uuid "2f21bf4f-8fb6-51cb-b46d-2704e33c0f5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241597} {:answer/id #uuid "6459af2f-a6b5-563c-b06a-268a7964e5fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242083} {:answer/id #uuid "a7a1a69e-9cf6-54d9-af78-f001be097a2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242650} {:answer/id #uuid "e0db43ec-63a9-52d8-940d-22db7f0c0613" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243178}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0db43ec-63a9-52d8-940d-22db7f0c0613" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243178} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfca5d8f-a9c0-5b49-923e-17f332033246" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243804}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4c2a663c-d7cd-55d2-8a80-b4c4edbb70bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-106765" :question/problem "Given that \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 3 \\\\ 14 & -5\\end{aligned}]$ \nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 3 \\\\ 14 & -5\\end{aligned}] & \\,{R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243953 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119793 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6093e24d-14bb-5e3b-a0b4-7a562a12f6a2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization of a Singular 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27a35bec-f769-5040-8918-554a7697cbed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241523} {:answer/id #uuid "36cc640e-8f40-5e5c-98c3-85377385c418" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241661} {:answer/id #uuid "5f1a9178-bb17-5d00-8b31-ca199e678655" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242035} {:answer/id #uuid "71edcb95-1b5d-5373-9c02-94ff15d39ff8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242191} {:answer/id #uuid "ea1fd436-d401-5a2d-823f-4992d3c757fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243253}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27a35bec-f769-5040-8918-554a7697cbed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241523} :answer-field/id #uuid "120d4359-9756-5178-aa7e-7692dc580a6b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243468}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "096e63c0-3ec3-55a0-bdd9-795c7447a69e" :question/math-academy-id "q-106773" :question/problem "Given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(\\frac{2}{3}){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-\\frac{2}{3},$ ${u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=-\\frac{2}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120182 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119792 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9279d979-74ba-5504-b98a-63692db3af72" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0c76f10-16e5-5123-ada9-c46ffc7d80fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117254} {:answer/id #uuid "dfc9837c-7702-5746-bde7-5a45cd35ac22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117255} {:answer/id #uuid "fa1b278a-4688-5c9f-be88-8efc4ef7dcca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117256} {:answer/id #uuid "368bae87-