/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402037} {:answer/id #uuid "fd6e6397-9397-52a6-ae26-dbbab796036e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402038} {:answer/id #uuid "4e9ea285-612d-5a27-90ce-9aac6b9d11bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402039} {:answer/id #uuid "683b97cc-4e24-5dae-871f-704c3c57f91e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402040} {:answer/id #uuid "936ed705-fd9f-5e57-9360-5bc6b86acdee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402041}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd6e6397-9397-52a6-ae26-dbbab796036e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402038} :answer-field/id #uuid "10f01866-5785-5c51-bd4f-76009cfa054f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402036}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "df166e56-b183-5016-9f58-5d60ce55786e" :question/math-academy-id "q-32546" :question/problem "Given that $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{aligned}]$ has the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120188 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119853 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c1a562-1b3d-5180-9fef-e20ea916b17a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying LU Factorizations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b0012c2-9e36-5ddc-b432-6223b1ea13ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117290} {:answer/id #uuid "d1f8da87-77c9-5d32-8a09-1e3610169e2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117291} {:answer/id #uuid "eb1b30fa-5a4e-5767-96e1-e58753e03a71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117292} {:answer/id #uuid "038ab4ad-a6fd-517d-9fa5-2542608f8143" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117293} {:answer/id #uuid "727efee5-a824-527e-82ff-d09ae7bd1bcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117294}] :answer-field/id #uuid "8a12d13e-2577-5a86-b53a-e88da174b659" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117289}] :question/id #uuid "85de5a76-468b-557f-8cba-f675615513a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-32551" :question/problem "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $A$?\n\n1. $A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n2. $A = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n3. $A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402054 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119792 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9279d979-74ba-5504-b98a-63692db3af72" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eab33316-9a2a-5e12-b51c-b02a826d1b0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402056} {:answer/id #uuid "1bad7312-2e66-5700-9aae-eb7c85b9c383" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402057} {:answer/id #uuid "7f572362-82aa-5062-a6d6-241eb9bbaaef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 18 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402058} {:answer/id #uuid "a48759ab-5bd7-525d-8ee8-1aa323ee45c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 32\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402059} {:answer/id #uuid "55aa56c5-1a06-51f3-9787-5f99d9d8585f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402060}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55aa56c5-1a06-51f3-9787-5f99d9d8585f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402060} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f4ecb30-087b-5664-9448-53b7642eb521" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402055}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c7e3bf80-d71d-502c-8977-ed540b710cc2" :question/math-academy-id "q-32608" :question/problem "Given that  $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -7\\end{aligned}]$ has the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot D \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 18\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 18\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302266 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119792 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9279d979-74ba-5504-b98a-63692db3af72" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4374f2ca-d187-5c74-a8e5-694af7f1a10f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300294} {:answer/id #uuid "4a359ac0-f626-5fc9-b0c1-01e620a91911" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300341} {:answer/id #uuid "7a50d87f-d58a-5c34-a7a5-6614e8291acf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300564} {:answer/id #uuid "c3a1b26d-8ffa-5c97-9580-8122a3e8e4fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "63" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300940} {:answer/id #uuid "d92efac6-01ae-5b2e-8354-9a9f798d247d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301039}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a359ac0-f626-5fc9-b0c1-01e620a91911" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300341} :answer-field/id #uuid "62bd16e6-f4e0-5d3c-b02e-ace76daef0c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301543}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "468d9e20-789c-5294-9d35-e95d404e4f0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-32617" :question/problem "Given that \n$B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 9 \\\\ 7 & 20\\end{aligned}]$ \nhas the LU decomposition $B=LU$, then ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{12}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $B$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $B$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 9 \\\\ 7 & 20\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{7}{3}){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{3} & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot B \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}B=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}B & =U \\\\ B & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ B & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{3} & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ B & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{7}{3} & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=\\frac{7}{3},{u}_{12}=9,$ and ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{12}=21.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345030 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119853 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c1a562-1b3d-5180-9fef-e20ea916b17a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying LU Factorizations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ada63ec-fa1d-5ff7-b669-92737dcdbd74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342330} {:answer/id #uuid "1752c8df-b894-5390-b173-aa620c63a4ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342443} {:answer/id #uuid "409dfa57-961d-5f83-aa15-10d15babbe22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342771} {:answer/id #uuid "467b7351-7ecb-539b-ba6e-6ea1995a8925" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342818} {:answer/id #uuid "98410b31-6e91-581a-a43b-5311f6c98db0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343573}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "467b7351-7ecb-539b-ba6e-6ea1995a8925" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342818} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c138955-72ce-5ce7-a05a-28705f2a9e68" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344682}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0305a43d-bf81-5ecb-8a77-49a30ade0147" :question/math-academy-id "q-75117" :question/problem "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $A?$\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $A,$ we write $A=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:  \n\n$$\nA=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the left is not a unit lower triangular matrix, as it has an entry not equal to $1$  on the main diagonal.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The second product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the right is not an upper triangular matrix, as it has a non-zero entry below the diagonal. \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The third product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}]}
